# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# Why this file exists:
#   1. Stockfish 16 NNUE multi-threading needs SharedArrayBuffer, which the
#      browser only enables when the page is "cross-origin isolated":
#        Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy:   same-origin
#        Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
#      Without these, the engine silently falls back to the single-threaded
#      build (`stockfish-nnue-16-single.js`) and analysis is ~3-4x slower.
#   2. With COEP=require-corp every subresource must opt-in via CORP. We mark
#      the engine + hashed Vite assets as same-origin so the worker can fetch
#      them.
#   3. Long-lived immutable caching for the engine + hashed asset bundles
#      means repeat visits load instantly from disk cache.
#
# If Clerk's sign-in widget breaks under `require-corp` (cross-origin script
# loads), swap that line to `credentialless` instead -- still enables
# SharedArrayBuffer on modern Chromium / Firefox, but is more permissive
# about cross-origin loads. Track the regression in DEPLOY.md.

/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=()

# Stockfish engine bundle. Files are pinned per-build so safe to mark
# immutable. If you swap Stockfish versions, change the filename or purge
# the CF cache for /stockfish/* explicitly.
#
# IMPORTANT: do NOT redeclare `Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy` here. The
# `/*` block above already sets it, and Cloudflare Pages *appends* (does
# not replace) per-route headers on top of the wildcard, so adding
# `COEP: require-corp` again produces a response with the header listed
# twice. Browsers reject worker scripts whose response has duplicate
# `Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy` headers — `new Worker(url)` then fails
# with an empty `ErrorEvent` (no message, no filename), which is exactly
# what we hit on the first prod deploy. We only need CORP here, since
# the wildcard `/*` block doesn't set CORP.
/stockfish/*
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Vite-hashed JS / CSS / image bundles (e.g. /assets/index-abc123.js).
# Vite changes the hash on every content change, so immutable is correct.
/assets/*
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# Puzzle corpus shards (e.g. /puzzles/7cff561b0a88/b12-0.tsv), ~50 files of
# ~360 KB fetched on demand by the Puzzles page.
#
# `Cache-Control` is the load-bearing line here. `immutable` is honest for
# these files because the path contains a content hash of the whole corpus
# (PUZZLE_BUILD_ID): refreshed data always lands on new URLs, so a cached
# shard can never be decoded against a newer theme vocabulary, and a returning
# user re-reads shards from disk cache instead of the network.
#
# CORP is defensive, NOT required — measured, not assumed. Under the
# `COEP: require-corp` set by the `/*` block above, a same-origin `fetch()` of
# a shard succeeds with or without this header (verified against a production
# build served with these exact headers: 200, full body, in both
# configurations). CORP is only enforced for CROSS-origin subresources, so the
# same-origin case passes the check either way. It is kept for consistency with
# the blocks above and so that moving the corpus to a separate asset host later
# doesn't silently start failing.
#
# The "do NOT redeclare COEP here" rule from the Stockfish block still applies:
# Cloudflare Pages appends per-route headers to the wildcard rather than
# replacing them, so repeating COEP would emit it twice.
/puzzles/*
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# index.html and any other top-level HTML must NOT be cached by the
# browser, otherwise users keep loading the previous deploy's bundle
# references after we ship a new build.
/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
/
  Cache-Control: no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate
